/// Subtract the mean.
///
/// Each value less the mean of the column, so that the result has mean zero and the spread of
/// the original: `x - mean(x)`. The mean is taken over the values present, with the reducers'
/// warning for the rows with `NA` skipped (section 4.1 of the Language Reference), and those rows
/// stay `NA` in the result. Centring a regressor before an interaction makes the main effects
/// read at the mean rather than at zero. Like R's `scale(x, scale = FALSE)`.
///
/// x: The column to centre.
/// returns: A `Column<Float>` with mean zero.
/// details: The mean is the compensated sum of the values present over their count; each value is then one subtraction away from it.
/// reference: R Core Team, the `scale` function of the base package, with `scale = FALSE`.
/// see also: standardize, mean, sd
/// > let d = frame(x = [2, 4, 9])
/// > center(d["x"])
/// Column<Float> · 3 values
/// -3
/// -1
/// 4
/// > d.add(xc = center(x))
/// frame · 3 rows × 2 variables
///
///       x   xc
///   1   2   -3
///   2   4   -1
///   3   9    4
fn center(x: Column<Float>) -> Column<Float> {
    x - frame(value = x).mean(value)
}
